@meta
id: 15-14
title: Copy, then change the copy
chapter: 15
type: lesson
skills: global-copy
par: 48
minutes: 6
vscode: copy the block, paste it, find-and-replace inside the selection
open: flags.fixture.ts

@brief
The feature flags fixture only seeds `staging`. Production needs the
same six flags with the same settings, so every `staging` row needs a
twin directly under it with `env: 'production'` and everything else
identical. The `ci` rows at the bottom are not part of this.

`:t.` copies a line to just below itself. On its own that gives you six
pairs of identical lines and a second job. But the command after a `:g`
pattern can be more than one Ex command; join them with a bar and they
run in turn on each marked line. After `:t.` the cursor is on the new
copy, so a substitute that follows it changes the copy and only the
copy.

@keys
| `:g/pattern/t.` | copy every matching line to directly below itself |
| `cmd1 | cmd2` | run two Ex commands one after the other |
| `:s/old/new/` | substitute on the current line, from chapter 14 |

@hints
Two steps per line, in one command: make the copy, then fix the copy.
--
`:t.` leaves the cursor on the copy. Whatever Ex command comes after the
bar runs there.
--
`:g/staging/t.|s/staging/production/` and Enter.

@solution
    :g/staging/t.|s/staging/production/<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Forty keystrokes for six new lines, each one correct on arrival.

Two things are happening and both are worth keeping.

The first is `:t.`: copy to here. With `:g` it duplicates every match in
place, which is the fixture-doubling move. Note that the copies are not
themselves marked, even though they match the pattern: marking happened
before anything ran. So `:g/staging/t.` produces exactly one copy of
each, never a runaway.

The second is the bar. `:g/pattern/A | B` runs `A` and then `B` on each
marked line, and the cursor position after `A` is where `B` starts. That
is what lets you copy and then edit the copy, or move a line and then
indent it, or delete a line and then substitute on the one that took
its place. It turns `:g` from "one command per match" into "one small
script per match", which is the last piece of the machine.

@start flags.fixture.ts
export const flags = [
  { flag: 'refund-window', env: 'staging', enabled: true },
  { flag: 'email-receipts', env: 'staging', enabled: true },
  { flag: 'partial-refunds', env: 'staging', enabled: false },
  { flag: 'order-notes', env: 'staging', enabled: true },
  { flag: 'gateway-v2', env: 'staging', enabled: false },
  { flag: 'customer-tiers', env: 'staging', enabled: true },
  { flag: 'refund-window', env: 'ci', enabled: true },
  { flag: 'gateway-v2', env: 'ci', enabled: true },
];

@expected flags.fixture.ts
export const flags = [
  { flag: 'refund-window', env: 'staging', enabled: true },
  { flag: 'refund-window', env: 'production', enabled: true },
  { flag: 'email-receipts', env: 'staging', enabled: true },
  { flag: 'email-receipts', env: 'production', enabled: true },
  { flag: 'partial-refunds', env: 'staging', enabled: false },
  { flag: 'partial-refunds', env: 'production', enabled: false },
  { flag: 'order-notes', env: 'staging', enabled: true },
  { flag: 'order-notes', env: 'production', enabled: true },
  { flag: 'gateway-v2', env: 'staging', enabled: false },
  { flag: 'gateway-v2', env: 'production', enabled: false },
  { flag: 'customer-tiers', env: 'staging', enabled: true },
  { flag: 'customer-tiers', env: 'production', enabled: true },
  { flag: 'refund-window', env: 'ci', enabled: true },
  { flag: 'gateway-v2', env: 'ci', enabled: true },
];
